1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,895
6 km under the sea, the water will squeeze this titanium ball — with almost 1,00,000 tonnes.

2
00:00:07,232 --> 00:00:10,594
Now imagine you're one of the three Indians sitting inside it.

3
00:00:10,923 --> 00:00:16,062
This ball is the heart of Matsya, India's first deep-sea sub for people.

4
00:00:16,300 --> 00:00:19,376
And between you and the sea, there are seven welds.

5
00:00:19,573 --> 00:00:22,709
If even one of them is weak, nothing else matters.

6
00:00:23,012 --> 00:00:24,556
Now here's the strange part.

7
00:00:24,809 --> 00:00:27,881
The hardest of those welds was made by ISRO.

8
00:00:28,210 --> 00:00:29,838
Yes, the rocket people.

9
00:00:30,127 --> 00:00:32,812
ISRO's hardest weld wasn't for a rocket.

10
00:00:32,928 --> 00:00:34,148
It was for this ball.

11
00:00:34,500 --> 00:00:37,630
Their machine could weld 2 cm of titanium.

12
00:00:37,861 --> 00:00:39,500
This wall is eight.

13
00:00:39,737 --> 00:00:42,003
It took them about 700 tries.

14
00:00:42,269 --> 00:00:43,745
So how did they do it?

15
00:00:44,013 --> 00:00:47,036
And after Titan, how will anyone know it holds?

16
00:00:47,318 --> 00:00:49,233
Stay with me, and you'll know both.

17
00:00:49,488 --> 00:00:53,147
You'll also see what this ball has to do with the phone in your hand.

18
00:00:53,533 --> 00:00:56,101
Hold out one finger, and look at the nail.

19
00:00:56,450 --> 00:01:02,258
Every 10 metres you go down, the sea presses on you as hard as all the air above you.

20
00:01:02,629 --> 00:01:04,073
10 metres, once.

21
00:01:04,270 --> 00:01:05,893
20 metres, twice.

22
00:01:06,105 --> 00:01:08,699
Now keep going, 600 times.

23
00:01:08,959 --> 00:01:15,513
At 6,000 metres, the water presses on that one fingernail with about 600 kilos.

24
00:01:15,735 --> 00:01:19,580
That's a full-grown buffalo, standing on your fingernail.

25
00:01:19,987 --> 00:01:24,360
Now cover the whole ball with fingernails, and put a buffalo on every one.

26
00:01:24,668 --> 00:01:25,538
Can you picture it?

27
00:01:25,817 --> 00:01:30,699
That's how you get almost 1,00,000 tonnes, from every side at once.

28
00:01:30,971 --> 00:01:32,382
I know what you want to ask me.

29
00:01:32,659 --> 00:01:34,746
Kishore, we've seen this story before.

30
00:01:34,916 --> 00:01:36,192
Is this another Titan?

31
00:01:36,577 --> 00:01:38,667
Think back to June 2023.

32
00:01:38,947 --> 00:01:41,969
Titan broke apart on its way down to the Titanic.

33
00:01:42,150 --> 00:01:43,348
Five people died.

34
00:01:43,640 --> 00:01:45,424
Its hull was carbon fibre.

35
00:01:45,669 --> 00:01:50,903
After earlier dives, its layers had started to come apart, and it kept diving anyway.

36
00:01:51,277 --> 00:01:54,921
The US Coast Guard said the company's own failures caused it.

37
00:01:55,186 --> 00:01:57,057
And one number has stayed with me.

38
00:01:57,358 --> 00:02:03,381
Where Titan broke, the sea was pressing a little more than half as hard as it will press on Matsya.

39
00:02:03,672 --> 00:02:05,791
So what is India doing differently?

40
00:02:06,168 --> 00:02:06,937
Three things.

41
00:02:07,188 --> 00:02:12,133
The metal is titanium, which deep-sea subs have trusted for more than 50 years.

42
00:02:12,357 --> 00:02:13,892
The shape is a ball.

43
00:02:14,143 --> 00:02:16,827
And the builders don't get to pass their own work.

44
00:02:16,899 --> 00:02:19,836
An outside inspection company has to approve it.

45
00:02:20,045 --> 00:02:22,694
For Matsya, that company is from Norway.

46
00:02:22,977 --> 00:02:25,349
And if something goes wrong while you're down there?

47
00:02:25,658 --> 00:02:29,137
The crew can drop heavy weights, and the sub rises back up.

48
00:02:29,308 --> 00:02:31,979
And you carry air for 96 hours.

49
00:02:32,223 --> 00:02:34,050
Squeeze a cricket ball in your fist.

50
00:02:34,210 --> 00:02:35,227
Nothing happens, right?

51
00:02:35,464 --> 00:02:37,636
The push spreads all the way round.

52
00:02:37,724 --> 00:02:41,231
There's no corner and no flat side where the pressure can win.

53
00:02:41,275 --> 00:02:45,916
That's why your cabin is a ball, and not a long tube like a normal submarine.

54
00:02:46,074 --> 00:02:50,209
Even America's famous deep-sea sub, Alvin, is built around a ball.

55
00:02:50,295 --> 00:02:53,142
Its first one, in 1964, was steel.

56
00:02:53,329 --> 00:02:58,902
In 1973, they changed it to titanium, and Alvin could go almost twice as deep.

57
00:02:59,008 --> 00:03:02,041
India walked the same road, one step at a time.

58
00:03:02,199 --> 00:03:08,068
In 2021, it lowered a test ball of plain steel 600 metres into the Bay of Bengal.

59
00:03:08,176 --> 00:03:09,628
Then came titanium.

60
00:03:09,746 --> 00:03:12,073
And titanium brought the real problem.

61
00:03:12,222 --> 00:03:15,561
You can't make a titanium ball this big in one piece.

62
00:03:15,715 --> 00:03:17,261
So how many pieces?

63
00:03:17,416 --> 00:03:19,519
ISRO's own drawing shows five.

64
00:03:19,722 --> 00:03:20,687
A top hatch.

65
00:03:20,758 --> 00:03:21,640
Three rings.

66
00:03:21,737 --> 00:03:22,739
A bottom dome.

67
00:03:22,840 --> 00:03:25,985
Together, about 5.5 tonnes of titanium.

68
00:03:26,143 --> 00:03:29,282
Five pieces are useless until someone joins them.

69
00:03:29,388 --> 00:03:31,113
That takes seven welds.

70
00:03:31,150 --> 00:03:33,721
Four go right round the ball, like belts.

71
00:03:33,780 --> 00:03:35,512
Three go round its windows.

72
00:03:35,629 --> 00:03:37,841
Now, titanium has one weakness.

73
00:03:37,900 --> 00:03:41,468
When it's hot, it pulls in oxygen and nitrogen from the air.

74
00:03:41,540 --> 00:03:43,662
The weld fills with tiny holes.

75
00:03:43,727 --> 00:03:45,902
It turns weak, and easy to crack.

76
00:03:45,986 --> 00:03:48,118
So a normal flame is no use here.

77
00:03:48,293 --> 00:03:49,160
What would you do?

78
00:03:49,304 --> 00:03:53,406
ISRO fired a beam of electrons instead, inside a vacuum chamber.

79
00:03:53,514 --> 00:03:55,448
No air, so nothing gets in.

80
00:03:55,563 --> 00:03:56,773
But there was a catch.

81
00:03:56,908 --> 00:04:00,788
ISRO's welding centre in Bengaluru could go 2 cm deep.

82
00:04:00,877 --> 00:04:01,971
This wall is eight.

83
00:04:01,971 --> 00:04:05,209
You might say, Kishore, then buy a bigger machine.

84
00:04:05,298 --> 00:04:07,313
They made their own machine stronger.

85
00:04:07,405 --> 00:04:10,037
From 15 kilowatts of power to 40.

86
00:04:10,190 --> 00:04:11,853
And then they practised.

87
00:04:11,952 --> 00:04:14,418
How many times, before they touched the real ball?

88
00:04:14,527 --> 00:04:15,622
Not ten times.

89
00:04:15,721 --> 00:04:16,652
Not a hundred.

90
00:04:16,757 --> 00:04:21,858
About 700 trial welds, until every setting was exactly right.

91
00:04:21,932 --> 00:04:25,441
Then came the real one, the weld your life will hang on.

92
00:04:25,529 --> 00:04:28,193
One of the big welds that go right round the ball.

93
00:04:28,273 --> 00:04:32,915
8 cm deep, and about 7 metres long, with no stopping halfway.

94
00:04:33,023 --> 00:04:34,156
How long did it take?

95
00:04:34,270 --> 00:04:35,668
32 minutes.

96
00:04:35,731 --> 00:04:36,690
One pass.

97
00:04:36,751 --> 00:04:41,245
ISRO says it was the first time anyone in India had made a weld like this.

98
00:04:41,266 --> 00:04:45,518
700 tries, for 32 minutes that had to be perfect.

99
00:04:45,585 --> 00:04:47,504
But how do you know it's good inside?

100
00:04:47,568 --> 00:04:49,216
You can't cut it open to check.

101
00:04:49,276 --> 00:04:54,243
So they made their X-ray machine strong enough to see through 8 cm of titanium.

102
00:04:54,291 --> 00:04:57,375
They used sound waves too, listening for hidden cracks.

103
00:04:57,441 --> 00:05:00,417
And then an outside agency has to certify it.

104
00:05:00,508 --> 00:05:02,052
Now step inside with me.

105
00:05:02,177 --> 00:05:07,156
The wall is 8 cm thick, so inside, you get just over 2 metres.

106
00:05:07,216 --> 00:05:10,916
Lie down, and your head and your feet would almost touch the walls.

107
00:05:10,977 --> 00:05:14,774
You and two others sit here for a normal dive of 12 hours.

108
00:05:14,870 --> 00:05:16,118
Oxygen comes in.

109
00:05:16,240 --> 00:05:19,168
The carbon dioxide you breathe out gets cleaned away.

110
00:05:19,360 --> 00:05:23,733
And India has already sent two of its own people into that darkness.

111
00:05:23,814 --> 00:05:28,199
In August 2025, they went down in a French sub called Nautile.

112
00:05:28,356 --> 00:05:32,128
Raju Ramesh, a scientist, reached about 4,000 metres.

113
00:05:32,217 --> 00:05:37,796
The next day, Commander Jatinder Pal Singh, a retired Navy officer, reached about 5,000.

114
00:05:37,935 --> 00:05:40,476
Each of them spent 7 hours down there.

115
00:05:40,687 --> 00:05:42,685
Two Indians have seen the deep.

116
00:05:42,868 --> 00:05:44,480
But you know the catch, don't you?

117
00:05:44,691 --> 00:05:47,339
They saw it from someone else's sub.

118
00:05:47,535 --> 00:05:49,320
Is Matsya ready to take them, then?

119
00:05:49,548 --> 00:05:50,237
Not yet.

120
00:05:50,450 --> 00:05:53,120
Let me be honest with you about what's still missing.

121
00:05:53,277 --> 00:05:55,384
Your ball is only part of the sub.

122
00:05:55,513 --> 00:05:59,239
Around it go batteries, lights, motors and arms.

123
00:05:59,314 --> 00:06:02,735
Put it all together, and the whole thing sinks like a stone.

124
00:06:02,862 --> 00:06:08,886
So it's wrapped in a special foam, full of tiny hollow glass bubbles set in hard plastic.

125
00:06:08,951 --> 00:06:10,828
The titanium takes the squeeze.

126
00:06:10,934 --> 00:06:12,537
The foam brings you home.

127
00:06:12,716 --> 00:06:14,603
The ball was welded in India.

128
00:06:14,708 --> 00:06:18,290
The foam had to come from France, and it arrived late.

129
00:06:18,369 --> 00:06:22,901
Then, early this year, officials said a small crack was found in its cover.

130
00:06:23,006 --> 00:06:25,365
And the sea has its own rule, as you know.

131
00:06:25,514 --> 00:06:28,605
You can't do this test in the monsoon, an official said.

132
00:06:28,698 --> 00:06:32,340
So the first test dive, to 500 metres, had to wait.

133
00:06:32,488 --> 00:06:38,217
On 6 November, Matsya finished another round of trials near Chennai, without people inside.

134
00:06:38,297 --> 00:06:43,014
Officials have said the full 6 km dive could come in 2027.

135
00:06:43,253 --> 00:06:44,626
Why go through all this?

136
00:06:44,765 --> 00:06:45,698
Take out your phone.

137
00:06:45,839 --> 00:06:48,897
Your battery needs metals like cobalt and nickel.

138
00:06:49,074 --> 00:06:52,073
Now come down with me, 6,000 metres.

139
00:06:52,199 --> 00:06:53,723
There's no sunlight at all.

140
00:06:53,850 --> 00:06:57,096
Your lights are the only lights for 6 km.

141
00:06:57,195 --> 00:07:00,441
Outside your window, the water is close to freezing.

142
00:07:00,543 --> 00:07:05,192
And the seafloor in front of you is covered with black lumps, like stones.

143
00:07:05,383 --> 00:07:06,479
What's inside them?

144
00:07:06,595 --> 00:07:09,758
Those lumps hold nickel, copper and cobalt.

145
00:07:09,884 --> 00:07:16,537
India has held the right to explore a big patch of that seabed, in the middle of the Indian Ocean, since 2002.

146
00:07:16,661 --> 00:07:22,008
In 2022, an Indian robot sub surveyed it, more than 5 km down.

147
00:07:22,177 --> 00:07:29,019
On Independence Day last year, the Prime Minister used a name you grew up with, for India's turn towards the deep sea.

148
00:07:29,146 --> 00:07:30,351
Samudra Manthan.

149
00:07:30,447 --> 00:07:32,130
The churning of the ocean.

150
00:07:32,288 --> 00:07:37,005
And India's Deep Ocean Mission has more than ₹4,000 crore behind it.

151
00:07:37,113 --> 00:07:38,389
But I'll be straight with you.

152
00:07:38,491 --> 00:07:40,786
Nobody is allowed to mine there yet.

153
00:07:40,910 --> 00:07:43,055
The world is still writing the rules.

154
00:07:43,179 --> 00:07:45,155
Matsya is not a mining machine.

155
00:07:45,362 --> 00:07:46,699
It's a pair of eyes.

156
00:07:46,920 --> 00:07:48,458
And what about China?

157
00:07:48,471 --> 00:07:54,513
Its sub has already taken people to the deepest point on Earth, almost 11,000 metres.

158
00:07:54,746 --> 00:07:58,241
So yes, India is behind, and you should know that.

159
00:07:58,447 --> 00:08:03,320
India's first goal is 6,000, deep enough for the seabed it wants to explore.

160
00:08:03,549 --> 00:08:06,685
But look at how India is getting there, before you judge.

161
00:08:06,909 --> 00:08:08,484
ISRO designed this ball.

162
00:08:08,818 --> 00:08:10,030
ISRO welded it.

163
00:08:10,289 --> 00:08:14,189
And when its machine wasn't strong enough, it made the machine stronger.

164
00:08:14,400 --> 00:08:17,742
Now, one last time, come back inside the ball.

165
00:08:18,070 --> 00:08:19,201
Would you go down today?

166
00:08:19,485 --> 00:08:22,438
Matsya has not been to sea with people in it yet.

167
00:08:22,586 --> 00:08:27,482
And no public report says this titanium ball has been tested at full pressure yet.

168
00:08:27,638 --> 00:08:30,351
So the last test will be the sea itself.

169
00:08:30,729 --> 00:08:31,514
One dive.

170
00:08:31,726 --> 00:08:32,973
Six kilometres.

171
00:08:33,115 --> 00:08:35,356
Three Indians, and seven welds.

172
00:08:35,537 --> 00:08:40,269
Under that much water, your ball is only as strong as its weakest weld.

173
00:08:40,528 --> 00:08:43,417
That's why 700 tries were not too many.

174
00:08:43,596 --> 00:08:45,774
So here's what you and I watch for.

175
00:08:45,938 --> 00:08:48,872
First, the test dive to 500 metres.

176
00:08:49,004 --> 00:08:50,584
Then, the big one.

177
00:08:50,765 --> 00:08:52,962
When Matsya goes down, we'll be there.

178
00:08:53,132 --> 00:08:54,871
Subscribe, so you're there too.

179
00:08:54,996 --> 00:08:56,295
And tell me in the comments.

180
00:08:56,418 --> 00:08:59,160
Is the deep sea worth this much effort for India?

181
00:08:59,335 --> 00:09:00,600
I read every one.

182
00:09:00,844 --> 00:09:02,618
This is Be Practical with Kishore.

183
00:09:02,770 --> 00:09:03,713
Stay practical.
